  "blocks": [],
  "meta": {
    "title": "Balanced parentheses with one counter",
    "blurb": "With only one kind of bracket, a stack is more than you need: the one fact worth keeping is how many are open. A counter that never goes below zero and ends at zero is exactly a balanced string — the Dyck language on one pair.",
    "inputs": [
      {
        "w": "",
        "expect": "accept",
        "label": "ε"
      },
      {
        "w": "()",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "(())",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "()(())",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "(",
        "expect": "reject"
      },
      {
        "w": ")(",
        "expect": "reject"
      },
      {
        "w": "())",
        "expect": "reject"
      },
      {
        "w": "(()",
        "expect": "reject"
      }
    ],
    "library": {
      "author": {
        "login": "thethinkmachine"
      },
      "license": "CC-BY-4.0",
      "tags": [
        "dyck-language",
        "brackets",
        "counting"
      ],
      "difficulty": "intro",
      "readme": "A one-counter automaton is a pushdown automaton whose stack alphabet is a single symbol above the bottom marker — so the stack is just a number, and the only question it can answer is \"is it zero?\".\n\nFor one kind of bracket that is enough: **(** increments, **)** decrements, and a **)** with the counter at zero has no move, which rejects the word. For two kinds — **( [ ] )** against **( [ ) ]** — the order of what is open matters, and a counter cannot remember order. See **Bracket matcher** among the PDAs for the version that needs a real stack."
    }
  }
}
